Name: Intel e1000 ethernet shared code (FreeBSD-derived)
License: BSD-3-Clause (see LICENSE)
License File: LICENSE
Upstream README: README.upstream

Provenance
==========

This directory vendors the OS-independent "shared code" (the hardware
abstraction layer: MAC/PHY/NVM bring-up for the whole 8254x / e1000e / I2xx
family) of the Intel ethernet driver.

Chain of custody:

  FreeBSD  sys/dev/e1000  (BSD-3-Clause, Intel + FreeBSD authors)
     |     original shared code, hash 3258eeeb442b48695165a6cb66aabd5b8422478c
     v
  Fuchsia  zircon/third_party/dev/ethernet/e1000
     |     a DFv1 port of the above. REMOVED from upstream Fuchsia before the
     |     Cerise vendored baseline; last present at Fuchsia commit
     |     171ed3ae9231. Fetched via the gitiles +archive endpoint:
     |       https://fuchsia.googlesource.com/fuchsia/+archive/171ed3ae9231/
     |         zircon/third_party/dev/ethernet/e1000.tar.gz
     v
  Cerise   src/cerise/drivers/e1000/third_party/e1000  (this directory)

What was vendored
=================

Only the OS-independent shared code is kept. Every file that is FreeBSD
"iflib" glue or Fuchsia DFv1 glue was intentionally dropped:

  DROPPED  fuchsia.c              -- DFv1 ddk/ethernet-banjo glue (replaced by
                                     the Cerise DFv2 driver in ../../e1000.cc)
  DROPPED  if_em.c, if_em.h       -- FreeBSD iflib driver (never built upstream)
  DROPPED  em_txrx.c, igb_txrx.c  -- FreeBSD iflib tx/rx (never built upstream)
  DROPPED  BUILD.gn               -- replaced by BUILD.bazel

Local modifications
===================

  e1000_osdep.h  -- REWRITTEN for DFv2. The upstream file bridged the shared
                    code to Fuchsia's DFv1 DDK (ddk/protocol/pci banjo,
                    ddk/mmio-buffer). The rewrite keeps the E1000_* register
                    macros byte-for-byte, but:
                      * register access goes through a plain MMIO base pointer
                        (`membase`), which the C++ driver sets to its
                        fdf::MmioBuffer virtual base;
                      * PCI config space goes through C-callable shims
                        (e1000_osdep_pci_read16/write16/find_cap) that the C++
                        driver implements against the fuchsia.hardware.pci FIDL
                        client;
                      * DEBUGOUT/DEBUGFUNC are compiled out (no printf in a
                        -nodefaultlibs driver .so);
                      * E1000_WRITE_REG_IO is routed to MMIO. Upstream used the
                        IO-mapped BAR only to dodge an 82540/82544 silicon
                        erratum on a CTRL.RST; the sole reachable caller here is
                        QEMU's emulated 82540EM, where MMIO reset is reliable and
                        no ioport resource is wired. Real targets (82574L / I219
                        / I211) never take that path.

  e1000_osdep.c  -- REWRITTEN: the six OS-specific PCI helpers
                    (e1000_read/write_pci_cfg, e1000_pci_set/clear_mwi,
                    e1000_read/write_pcie_cap_reg) now call the C shims above.

No other vendored file was modified; they compile unchanged (warnings
suppressed via -w, as upstream built them under -Wno-conversion).
